  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nNorthwest flow aloft with an axis of surface high pressure from the\nLower MS Valley thru the Ohio Valley. This has resulted in mainly\nclear skies and light winds. Temperatures dipped down to lows in the\nthe upper 20s to the lower 30s. With readings warming up - have\nallowed the freeze warning to expire at 10 AM. Highs expected to\nreach the upper 50s this afternoon under abundant sunshine. \n\nMid level short wave drops thru the eastern Great Lakes today into\ntonight. A weak associated trof/front slides into the region\ntonight and falls apart. Ahead of this front only expect some high \nlevel clouds and a tightening of the pressure gradient with southwest\nwinds gusting up to 25 to 30 mph - mainly north of I-70. \n\nOnly expect a few scattered clouds with the front with surface high\npressure nosing into the southwest late tonight. Although\ntemperatures will not be quite as cold as this morning lows in the\nmid to upper 30s will lead to the formation of frost. Therefore, have\nissued a frost advisory for tonight thru 9 AM Monday morning. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nMid level flow will back more westerly to start the week with \nridging at the surface and aloft offering dry conditions and above\nnormal temperatures. Highs in the mid 50s to around 60 Monday, to the\nlower and middle 60s Tuesday, and then within a few degrees of 60 \nWednesday.  \n\nA digging system will result in the passage of a surface cold front \nlate Wednesday into Thursday. This system leads to a mid level low \nthat develops over the Great Lakes late in the week and continues to\ninfluence our weather into the first part of the weekend.  \n\nThis will lead to a chance of rain beginning Wednesday night and \ncontinuing into Thursday. The mid level low will keep the threat for \nprecipitation in the forecast into Friday and perhaps Saturday.  \n\nTemperatures will turn colder with highs in the 50s Thursday, the \n40s for Friday, and the upper 40s to around 50 Saturday.",
